<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

$q = static fn(string $difficulty, string $stem, string $answer, array $distractors, string $explanation, string $competency, string $scenario): array => [
    'difficulty'=>$difficulty, 'question'=>$stem, 'answer'=>$answer, 'distractors'=>$distractors,
    'explanation'=>$explanation, 'tested_competency'=>$competency, 'scenario'=>$scenario,
];

return [
    'pe-fundamental-movement-analysis'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner can move toward a target but repeatedly steps outside the marked lane. Which observation lens best identifies the issue?','Examine the learner’s pathway and spatial relationship to the lane.',['Measure the learner’s heart rate after the activity.','Assess the learner’s ability to name the target color.','Compare the learner’s uniform with classmates’ uniforms.'],'Pathway and space are movement concepts that directly relate to staying within the lane.','Analyze movement using task-relevant spatial features.','marked-lane-pathway'),
        $q('medium','A teacher observes a learner throw with good balance but inconsistent force. Which feedback focus is most useful?','Use a cue related to effort or force and observe the next attempt.',['Correct the learner’s entire throwing sequence at once.','Change the target size before identifying the source of inconsistency.','Ask the learner to run faster before each throw.'],'Effort or force is the movement feature most directly connected to the observed inconsistency.','Select a movement cue based on observed performance.','throw-force-feedback'),
        $q('medium','A learner weaves through cones successfully while stationary but loses control when a partner moves nearby. What feature has changed?','The movement relationship to another person and a changing space demand.',['The learner’s knowledge of the cone colors.','The floor texture, even though the surface is unchanged.','The learner’s ability to count repetitions.'],'A moving partner changes spatial relationships and requires the learner to adapt movement.','Analyze how relationships alter movement demands.','moving-partner-cones'),
        $q('medium','In a balance task, a learner stays upright on a wide base but wobbles when the base narrows. What is a useful next teaching focus?','Let the learner compare a manageable base of support and notice how body position affects balance.',['Increase speed so the learner has less time to notice the movement.','Add equipment before the learner can control the simpler task.','Score balance only by comparing the learner with the most stable peer.'],'The changed base offers evidence about balance; a manageable progression helps the learner explore control.','Use a task feature to develop movement awareness.','balance-base'),
        $q('medium','A learner’s movement becomes less accurate when a ball is larger than the one used in practice. What should the teacher analyze?','How the object changes the movement relationship and whether practice should progress across object sizes.',['Whether the learner memorized the activity name.','Whether the learner should stop practicing with objects.','Whether the class should use only the largest ball.'],'The object and body relationship changes the task demands; varied, planned practice can support adaptation.','Analyze movement with objects and changing conditions.','ball-size-change'),
        $q('hard','A student can perform a movement pattern in a straight line but not when asked to change direction on a cue. Which learning focus is most relevant?','Practice the same movement with controlled direction changes and attend to space and timing cues.',['Replace the pattern with an unrelated endurance task.','Increase the number of cues before the student practices.','Assess only whether the student remembers the pattern’s name.'],'The learner needs to apply the pattern under a changing space and timing demand.','Progress from stable movement to a more complex movement problem.','direction-cue-pattern'),
    ],
    'pe-motor-learning-feedback'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner’s landing is unstable after a jump. Which cue is most useful for another practice attempt?','“Land quietly with control, then hold your balance.”',['“Try harder and do everything better.”','“Look at the scoreboard while you land.”','“Jump as far as possible on every attempt.”'],'A concise cue directs attention to a controllable feature linked to the observed skill.','Use specific feedback to guide another movement attempt.','jump-landing-cue'),
        $q('medium','A student misses a target repeatedly, and the teacher notices that the release point varies. What feedback should come first?','Give one cue about a consistent release point and observe whether accuracy changes.',['Correct the student’s stance, grip, speed, and follow-through at once.','Tell the student the target is too difficult without checking the release.','Demonstrate the whole skill again without identifying the observed variation.'],'One focused cue helps the learner attend to the feature most related to the difficulty.','Use observation to select a high-value cue.','variable-release-point'),
        $q('medium','A learner performs a skill better after a demonstration than after a verbal explanation alone. What should the teacher plan?','Combine a brief visible model with a clear cue, then allow practice and observe performance.',['Use only more verbal explanation because modeling takes time.','Show the model repeatedly without allowing practice.','Assess whether the learner can describe the model rather than perform the skill.'],'A model can make movement visible, but practice is needed to assess skill learning.','Match instruction to movement learning and performance evidence.','model-before-practice'),
        $q('medium','A learner receives several corrections before each attempt and becomes unsure which to use. What should the teacher change?','Select one priority cue, let the learner practice, and add another cue only if needed.',['Increase the number of corrections so the learner has more information.','Stop giving feedback throughout the unit.','Have a peer give a different cue before every attempt.'],'Too many cues can overload attention; a focused cue supports purposeful practice.','Sequence feedback to maintain learner attention.','multiple-cue-overload'),
        $q('medium','A group improves a passing drill when the target is made visible. How should the teacher use this evidence?','Keep the visual target while varying one manageable condition and observe whether accuracy transfers.',['Remove the target immediately and assume the skill is learned.','Keep the drill unchanged for the remainder of the unit.','Change multiple conditions before checking the current learning.'],'A visual target helps; gradual variation tests transfer while keeping the task interpretable.','Use practice evidence to adjust task complexity.','visible-passing-target'),
        $q('hard','A learner succeeds only after a teacher repeats the cue during every movement. What should the teacher do to check independent learning?','Gradually reduce the cue and observe whether the learner can perform under the intended task conditions.',['Keep repeating the cue indefinitely and record each attempt as independent.','Stop all practice because the learner has not learned the skill.','Have a classmate move the learner’s body into position.'],'Fading prompts reveals whether the learner can use the skill with less support.','Check transfer as prompts are reduced.','fading-movement-cue'),
    ],
    'pe-fitness-component-interpretation'=>[
        $q('easy','A lesson objective concerns muscular endurance. Which activity evidence best matches that construct?','Observe repeated controlled muscle actions over the stated task conditions.',['Measure a single short sprint time.','Assess how far a learner can reach while seated.','Record how accurately the learner names a sports rule.'],'Repeated controlled muscular work is more directly related to endurance than sprint speed or flexibility.','Match evidence to a health-related fitness component.','muscular-endurance-task'),
        $q('medium','A teacher uses a flexibility measure to make a claim about cardiorespiratory endurance. What is the problem?','The measure does not match the fitness component named in the claim.',['The measure includes too much movement for a PE class.','The measure should be used only during team games.','The claim is valid whenever a learner completes a test.'],'Assessment evidence should represent the construct the teacher claims to measure.','Check construct-to-measure alignment.','flexibility-endurance-confusion'),
        $q('medium','A learner records a longer distance in a running activity but says the task felt easier after several sessions. Which fitness interpretation is reasonable?','The repeated activity may provide evidence related to endurance, but conditions and other evidence should be considered.',['The result proves every health-related fitness component improved.','The result establishes a medical diagnosis.','The result should be compared only with the fastest classmate.'],'A task can inform one aspect of fitness, but it does not support broad or clinical conclusions.','Interpret fitness evidence within its limits.','repeated-running-evidence'),
        $q('medium','A class objective concerns balance, but the selected test records how quickly learners sprint between markers. What needs to change?','Select an assessment task that actually elicits balance performance.',['Keep the sprint because every fitness measure assesses balance.','Add a written quiz about balance and ignore movement evidence.','Score only participation in the sprint.'],'The test must provide evidence for the named skill or fitness component.','Choose movement evidence that matches the objective.','balance-sprint-mismatch'),
        $q('medium','A learner asks why a body-composition result does not show how well they coordinate a movement pattern. What is the best explanation?','Each measure represents a particular construct; movement coordination requires its own relevant performance evidence.',['All fitness measures indicate the same ability.','Coordination cannot be assessed through movement.','The learner should use the result to judge personal worth.'],'Different measures address different constructs; one should not be treated as a proxy for another.','Distinguish fitness constructs and skill-related capacities.','body-composition-coordination'),
        $q('hard','A teacher is choosing among several tests for an age-specific fitness unit. What should guide the choice?','The intended fitness component, learner group, current applicable criteria, and safe administration conditions.',['Which test creates the widest score range.','Which test is easiest to rank from highest to lowest.','Which test is familiar even if it measures a different component.'],'Valid selection depends on construct, learner group, applicable standards, and safe conditions.','Select an appropriate criterion-referenced fitness assessment.','fitness-test-selection'),
    ],
    'pe-developmentally-safe-progression'=>[
        $q('easy','Learners are new to a throwing skill. Which sequence provides a manageable progression?','Begin with a stable, close target and change one task demand after learners show control.',['Start with the farthest target and the smallest available ball.','Add speed, distance, and moving defense in the first attempt.','Assess full-game performance before introducing the skill.'],'A stable starting task lets learners practice control before added complexity.','Sequence practice from manageable toward complex demands.','throwing-progression'),
        $q('medium','A class uses repeated high-effort circuits but has little time to recover between rounds. What is a sound teacher adjustment?','Review the activity demands and provide an appropriate pace and recovery before repeating the circuit.',['Increase the number of rounds to help learners adapt faster.','Keep the schedule because all learners should work at the same pace.','Remove all recovery so the activity remains vigorous.'],'Safe progression considers demand and recovery; a teacher should adjust conditions to the learner group.','Plan physical activity with manageable demand and recovery.','circuit-recovery'),
        $q('medium','A game becomes chaotic when learners must run, pass, and defend at once. What should the teacher try?','Reduce players or space demands and focus on one decision while preserving the game objective.',['Add more rules before learners understand the current task.','End all game practice and return only to lecture.','Require faster play so learners adapt to the challenge.'],'A smaller task can preserve the learning problem while reducing complexity and improving practice.','Adjust task complexity to support development and safety.','chaotic-small-game'),
        $q('medium','A learner reports discomfort during a new movement activity. What should the teacher do?','Pause the learner’s participation and follow the school’s established response process.',['Encourage the learner to continue so the teacher can finish the lesson.','Ask classmates to decide whether the discomfort is serious.','Tell the learner to self-diagnose and return when ready.'],'The teacher should not diagnose or minimize symptoms; school response procedures guide next steps.','Respond to learner concerns through school safety procedures.','discomfort-during-activity'),
        $q('medium','A warm outdoor surface becomes slippery after rain before a running activity. What is the best decision?','Reassess the surface and choose a safer activity space or task before learners begin.',['Keep the planned running route because it was inspected earlier.','Ask learners to run more slowly on the surface without checking it.','Continue because the activity is not scored.'],'Environmental conditions can change; the teacher should inspect and adjust before activity.','Recheck environmental safety before participation.','rain-surface-change'),
        $q('hard','A class includes learners with different levels of experience in a balance sequence. What progression best supports all learners?','Offer stable starting options and increase challenge through a planned choice of base, direction, or movement.',['Use the most complex version for everyone to ensure equal treatment.','Keep the simplest version for every learner regardless of progress.','Rank learners and assign permanent versions by initial score.'],'Differentiated progression keeps a shared goal while allowing appropriate, safe challenge.','Adapt progression to learner readiness and evidence.','balance-sequence-differentiation'),
    ],
    'pe-valid-fitness-assessment'=>[
        $q('easy','A PE teacher wants to assess whether learners can perform a movement pattern taught in class. Which evidence is most appropriate?','Observe each learner demonstrate the pattern under clear, age-appropriate conditions.',['Use a written quiz on the pattern name alone.','Compare learners’ body size with a chart.','Ask peers which learner looks most athletic.'],'Direct performance observation aligns with a movement skill outcome. Clear conditions let the teacher see the taught actions and give feedback on the relevant criteria.','Use developmentally appropriate movement evidence.','movement-pattern-observation'),
        $q('medium','A teacher compares a learner’s fitness score with a chart from a different age group. What should be corrected?','Use the current criterion that applies to the learner group and the specific test.',['Keep the chart because all fitness scores are interchangeable.','Use the highest available standard to challenge the learner.','Replace the test with a class ranking.'],'The PRC scope calls for applying the relevant fitness criteria. A chart for a different group may change the meaning of the comparison, so confirm the test and applicable current criteria before interpreting the result.','Match criteria to learner group and assessment.','wrong-age-criterion'),
        $q('medium','A test is administered outdoors on a windy day even though it is usually conducted indoors. What should the teacher document?','The changed testing conditions and whether they may affect interpretation of the result.',['Only the learner’s final score.','The class average without mentioning the conditions.','A health conclusion based on the score.'],'Conditions matter when interpreting performance; document changes and avoid unsupported claims.','Interpret evidence in light of assessment conditions.','outdoor-test-condition'),
        $q('medium','A teacher wants to assess progress in a skill unit. Which plan gives more useful evidence than one final test?','Use initial, during-unit, and final skill evidence aligned to the outcome.',['Use one final score and infer the learner’s starting point from that result.','Use weekly class ranks without criterion-linked feedback.','Use learner self-ratings as the only evidence of movement skill.'],'Diagnostic, formative, and summative evidence can guide instruction and summarize learning. Each check should align to the outcome and give the teacher information for a next instructional step.','Use multiple assessment points for learning decisions.','skill-unit-assessment-cycle'),
        $q('medium','A learner’s score changes between two fitness checks, but the test instructions differed. What is the strongest next step?','Review administration conditions and use a consistent, applicable method before comparing results.',['Assume the score change proves a lasting fitness change.','Discard every fitness result from the class.','Choose whichever score better matches expectations.'],'A comparison requires attention to whether the test was administered consistently.','Check assessment consistency before interpreting change.','different-test-instructions'),
        $q('hard','A learner’s performance is below the class median on a fitness task. How should the teacher use the result?','Interpret it against the applicable criterion and use it privately to guide an achievable learning step.',['Label the learner as unfit based on the class median.','Display the score so peers can motivate the learner.','Assume the result reflects effort rather than the task conditions.'],'Class rank alone is not a valid educational judgment; criterion-based evidence can guide instruction while protecting dignity.','Use fitness results constructively and appropriately.','below-median-fitness-result'),
    ],
    'pe-games-tactical-understanding'=>[
        $q('easy','In a small-sided invasion game, learners crowd around the ball and leave space open. What tactical idea should the teacher emphasize?','Move to useful open space to create a passing option.',['Run toward the ball regardless of teammate positions.','Wait at the boundary until the teacher gives a new rule.','Keep the ball with one player for the entire round.'],'Moving into open space creates a visible passing option and helps spread players across the available space. The teacher can then observe whether learners use that space to support the ball carrier.','Use game evidence to teach a tactical principle.','open-space-invasion-game'),
        $q('medium','A team can pass accurately in pairs but loses possession in a game. Which practice is most relevant?','Use a small-sided game that adds a manageable defender and asks learners to decide when and where to pass.',['Repeat stationary pairs only for the rest of the unit.','Practice sprinting without a ball.','Use a full-class game with no feedback or adaptation.'],'A game-like task bridges technique and tactical decisions while controlling complexity.','Connect skill practice with game decisions.','pair-pass-game-transfer'),
        $q('medium','During a target game, learners throw toward the first target they see rather than considering the score and turn. What question can improve decision-making?','Which target gives your team the best opportunity on this turn, and what information supports that choice?',['Who can throw the hardest from the longest distance.','Which target color is most attractive.','Why should every player select the same target.'],'The question prompts learners to reason about game state and strategy rather than only execution.','Use game context to support tactical reasoning.','target-game-decision'),
        $q('medium','A team has difficulty maintaining possession in a passing game. What adjustment can help learners practice support?','Reduce the playing area or number of defenders and cue teammates to move into passing lanes.',['Increase the number of defenders to pressure the team further.','Remove passing from the game.','Score only the fastest player’s movement.'],'A modified game can make support and passing-lane decisions visible while preserving the tactical goal.','Use task constraints to teach support play.','possession-support'),
        $q('medium','A learner performs a good pass but immediately watches the ball instead of moving. Which feedback is most relevant?','Cue the learner to move into a new supporting space after passing.',['Correct the pass even though it reached the teammate.','Ask the learner to stay still until the play ends.','Focus on the learner’s uniform color.'],'The observed gap is an off-ball tactical action, not the completed pass.','Differentiate execution feedback from tactical feedback.','pass-and-move'),
        $q('hard','A team repeatedly chooses an open target that is too far away for the current skill level. What should the teacher prompt?','Compare the available targets with the team’s current range and select a reachable strategic option.',['Choose the farthest target to show confidence.','Avoid discussing strategy and focus only on speed.','Have the teacher choose every target for the team.'],'Tactical decisions should account for both game conditions and learners’ current performance capabilities.','Integrate strategy with realistic skill evidence.','target-range-strategy'),
    ],
    'pe-rhythmic-movement-and-dance'=>[
        $q('easy','Learners move to a steady beat using a repeated two-step pattern. What should the teacher observe?','Whether learners coordinate the pattern with the beat and maintain the sequence.',['Whether learners choose the same color clothing.','Whether the pattern matches a teacher’s preferred style.','Whether learners can name every instrument in the room.'],'The intended evidence is the timing and order of the movement. Observing those features shows whether learners connect the repeated pattern to the steady beat.','Assess rhythm and movement sequence.','two-step-beat'),
        $q('medium','A dance phrase feels uneven because learners start at different counts. What support can help?','Use a clear count-in and practice the opening cue before repeating the phrase.',['Increase the tempo so the phrase finishes faster.','Remove the rhythm and have learners improvise separately.','Score learners on facial expression before timing is practiced.'],'A shared count-in gives the group a common start cue. Rehearsing that cue first helps isolate the timing problem before the class repeats the full phrase.','Use rhythm cues to support coordinated performance.','uneven-dance-start'),
        $q('medium','A class creates a movement sequence using high and low levels. Which prompt develops movement concepts?','How does changing level alter the shape and space used in the sequence?',['Which learner reaches the highest level.','Why should every movement stay at one level.','How many shoes are visible in the dance space.'],'Level and shape are observable movement qualities that connect to space and expression.','Analyze movement qualities in a dance sequence.','high-low-levels'),
        $q('medium','A learner keeps the beat but cannot remember a long dance sequence. What is a useful teaching adjustment?','Teach the sequence in short phrases and connect them with a consistent transition cue.',['Repeat the full sequence faster until it is memorized.','Remove the rhythm so learners can focus only on the teacher.','Have a peer perform while the learner watches each time.'],'Short phrases reduce memory load while the learner practices the full movement sequence progressively.','Sequence movement practice in manageable parts.','long-dance-sequence'),
        $q('medium','Learners create different movement responses to the same musical phrase. What should guide a fair assessment?','Use the taught criteria for rhythm, movement quality, and interpretation rather than requiring identical choreography.',['Score only the response that copies the teacher exactly.','Rank movements by how familiar they look.','Ignore the musical phrase and assess costume design.'],'Learners can express a shared prompt in varied ways while meeting the same learning criteria.','Assess creative movement against shared criteria.','varied-movement-responses'),
        $q('hard','A dance sequence includes a rapid turn that some learners cannot safely perform in the available space. What is the strongest design response?','Adapt the turn or spacing, preserving the rhythm goal and safe participation.',['Keep the original turn and change speed only after a learner loses balance.','Replace all movement assessment with a written reflection.','Preserve the turn by shortening the sequence without checking space or access.'],'The teacher can preserve the learning intention while adapting movement and space to conditions. The revised task should still provide evidence of the intended rhythm or movement concept.','Adapt choreography for safety and access.','rapid-turn-space'),
    ],
    'pe-inclusive-activity-design'=>[
        $q('easy','A target game requires a long throw, but several learners are not yet accurate at that distance. What adaptation best supports learning?','Offer closer targets or lighter equipment while keeping the aiming decision.',['Remove aiming from the activity for those learners.','Score only the strongest throw.','Have the teacher throw for each learner.'],'The adjustment changes access and difficulty while preserving the target skill.','Adapt equipment or distance to maintain meaningful practice.','target-distance-adaptation'),
        $q('medium','A learner with limited hand mobility is excluded from a passing game because the rules require a specific grip. What should the teacher consider?','Ask the learner about a workable option and adjust the equipment or pass rule while preserving the passing goal.',['Keep the rule unchanged because fairness means identical equipment.','Assign the learner as a scorekeeper without asking.','Remove the passing objective for the entire class.'],'Meaningful adaptation can preserve the shared goal and include learner input.','Design an accessible participation route.','limited-hand-mobility-game'),
        $q('medium','A large class cannot safely use the full court for running drills. What is a useful activity redesign?','Divide learners into smaller stations with clear pathways and supervision.',['Keep all learners running across the whole court at once.','Ask learners to move around obstacles without changing the layout.','Cancel movement practice and use only a written explanation.'],'Stations can reduce crowding and improve access and safety while keeping learners active.','Adapt space and grouping to the class.','crowded-court-stations'),
        $q('medium','A game relies on visual signals that one learner cannot access consistently. What change can support participation?','Add a clear sound or tactile cue while keeping the same start-and-stop rules.',['Remove the learner from the game.','Ask another learner to move the learner whenever a signal occurs.','Make the visual signal smaller so everyone focuses.'],'A second cue can make game information accessible while preserving the rules and learner agency.','Provide an accessible signal for shared activity.','visual-signal-access'),
        $q('medium','A team activity lets only the fastest learners touch the ball. Which redesign supports equitable participation?','Use a rule or task constraint that creates more opportunities for each learner to receive and pass.',['Keep the same pattern because it rewards the strongest players.','Remove the ball from the activity.','Assign slower learners to wait outside the play area.'],'A task constraint can distribute practice opportunities and preserve the team decision goal.','Design participation opportunities into game rules.','team-touch-opportunities'),
        $q('hard','A learner can participate in a striking game when the ball is stationary but not when it is moving quickly. What should the teacher do?','Begin with the stationary-ball version, then increase movement speed as the learner shows control.',['Use only the fastest moving ball to match the formal game.','Remove the striking skill and ask the learner to observe.','Keep the learner on the easiest version permanently.'],'A planned progression makes the skill accessible while retaining a route toward the shared game challenge.','Use graduated adaptation based on performance evidence.','striking-speed-progression'),
    ],
    'pe-injury-prevention-and-response'=>[
        $q('easy','Before class, a teacher finds loose equipment on the planned activity path. What is the best next step?','Clear the hazard or choose another safe space before learners begin.',['Ask learners to avoid the equipment while moving quickly.','Begin the activity and monitor whether anyone trips.','Move only the equipment that is easy to reach.'],'Inspection and correction before activity reduce foreseeable hazards. The teacher should make the movement space clear before participation, or move the activity to a suitable area.','Prevent injury by checking the activity environment.','loose-equipment-path'),
        $q('medium','A learner says they feel dizzy during a vigorous activity. What should the teacher do?','Stop the learner’s activity and follow the school’s established response procedure.',['Ask the learner to finish the round and report back.','Tell the learner the feeling is normal for exercise.','Have classmates decide whether the learner should continue.'],'The teacher should not diagnose or encourage continuing through symptoms; use school response procedures.','Respond safely to learner symptoms without giving medical advice.','dizziness-during-activity'),
        $q('medium','Several students slip during a game after the floor becomes wet. What should the teacher do?','Stop play, address the surface hazard, and resume only when the space is suitable.',['Continue but remove points for falling.','Ask students to run more carefully without changing the surface.','Move the game equipment while leaving the wet area in use.'],'A changed surface requires reassessment and correction before activity resumes.','Respond to changing environmental hazards.','wet-floor-game'),
        $q('hard','A minor injury occurs during a class activity, and learners gather around. What is the PE teacher’s priority?','Pause the activity, protect the learner’s privacy, and follow the school’s first-aid or emergency procedure.',['Ask the learner to continue so the class can finish.','Let peers decide what care is needed.','Discuss the injury publicly while continuing the game.'],'The teacher should use established school response procedures and protect the learner’s dignity. The teacher should avoid public discussion or diagnosis and follow the school’s designated first-aid or emergency process.','Follow school procedures for injury response.','injury-class-response'),
    ],
];
